        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Directing Amoriem Labs",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Over winter break of my first year at Yale, I paused in the middle of watching a playthrough of Toby Fox’s Deltarune, the sequel to his internet megahit indie game, Undertale. Toby Fox became a sort of legend in the game development world as the sole creator of Undertale, a college student who somehow had the coding, musical, artistic, storytelling, and marketing prowess to create one of the most influential indie games of all time.",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "type": "image",
      "moduleId": null,
      "image": {
        "role": "content",
        "sourceUrl": "https://cdn.myportfolio.com/c7e93eda-a4d0-46e4-920f-c5a1915a4d2c/522f5302-ebf6-43d7-a0c9-806310ccd0ba.gif?h=219f07afdf149c88d895aaa02c864452",
        "variant": "lightbox-original",
        "declaredWidth": null,
        "crop": null,
        "assetId": "522f5302-ebf6-43d7-a0c9-806310ccd0ba",
        "ext": "gif",
        "alt": "",
        "width": 636,
        "height": null,
        "caption": null
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "caption",
          "text": "Toby Fox breaks all normal bounds of games in Undertale. His game somehow keeps track of all your decisions, using each time you \"save\" your game not just as a user feature but also as a story plotpoint.",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Undertale sold over 3.5 million copies worldwide. One of the game’s main characters is currently playable in Super Smash Bros. And Toby Fox recently contributed music to Pokemon Sword/Shield as a guest composer.\n\nI wondered whether us students at Yale had that same talent and ingenuity. The answer came immediately — we undeniably did, but alone I certainly could not accomplish what Toby did.\n\nSo I sent this email to our CS majors panlist, with one of my main worries being my then-lack of programming experience.",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "type": "image",
      "moduleId": null,
      "image": {
        "role": "content",
        "sourceUrl": "https://cdn.myportfolio.com/c7e93eda-a4d0-46e4-920f-c5a1915a4d2c/aad2f810-9531-4593-abf6-e780cd328a36.jpg?h=e33ed3e74be2c30d03615b1b63eef234",
        "variant": "lightbox-original",
        "declaredWidth": null,
        "crop": null,
        "assetId": "aad2f810-9531-4593-abf6-e780cd328a36",
        "ext": "jpg",
        "alt": "",
        "width": 1864,
        "height": null,
        "caption": null
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "I got over twenty responses from people saying they were so surprised that a Yale game studio had never been created before, and that they’d love to contribute their various skills. I hosted meetings every week in the spring semester, testing out our skills in programming, digital art, animation, music composition, storytelling, and game design, and planning a proof of concept game release for Bulldog Days, Yale’s pre-frosh gathering in April.\n\nA snapshot of the game we ended up making:",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "type": "image",
      "moduleId": null,
      "image": {
        "role": "content",
        "sourceUrl": "https://cdn.myportfolio.com/c7e93eda-a4d0-46e4-920f-c5a1915a4d2c/2f3254c0-8c27-48b6-9073-405eb88280fc.gif?h=bca32c3131ef2479ce955d9dd2d58694",
        "variant": "lightbox-original",
        "declaredWidth": null,
        "crop": null,
        "assetId": "2f3254c0-8c27-48b6-9073-405eb88280fc",
        "ext": "gif",
        "alt": "",
        "width": 600,
        "height": null,
        "caption": null
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "caption",
          "text": "Our game featured local multiplayer, meaning two people can each control a character, one with WASD and the other with arrow keys. We created all assets from scratch, including the character sprites and animations!",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "type": "text",
      "moduleId": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "What we came up with was Amoriem, which is an anagram of memoria (“memory” in Latin) and includes the words amor (“love” in Spanish) and mori (“to die” in Latin), the three central themes of our game. This game was a dungeon-based local multiplayer game where two characters work together to escape levels.\n\nTheir story, slowly revealed through gameplay, was that they were twins involved in a car crash trying to regain their memories. The twist was that, despite needing to collaborate to finish each level, only one person could escape the final level and survive their \"coma.\"\n\nWe coded this demo using Unity Game Engine, which uses C#. To compose maps from map tiles, we used Tiled Map Editor, and for all asset creation we used Adobe Photoshop.",
          "links": []
        }
      ],
      "links": []
    },
